Z||000005||stimpy||01-01-2001||05:27 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Philphine:<BR><B>stimpy i thought you had plus. can you see for example, the "show us your interior thread"? if you can't get it with plus i think i'll skip it.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Naw, I've got the old-school Magnavox big box. Not even the "Classic". Just plain ol' WebTV.  I have come up with short threads that don't come up. I think if someone posts a bitmap image that totally screws the pooch. <BR>As far as the TV programmability, you can get all that plus 211 channels with digital cable. For about $40 a month.||||reg||1
Z||000006||fiero56||01-01-2001||05:31 PM||||I'd put it off for a computer. I've never used webtv, but from what you guys have said about them, I'd get a computer. I didn't know you couldn't get big threads opened. 10 minutes?!<P>I was just out at Circuit City(not my choice to buy a computer but they have them) and they had one with a Celeron 633, 128MB ram, 40x cd-rom, etc. It was only $350 after rebate, and monitor was another $150. Sure, it's not the best you can buy(probably no room for expansion, etc), but if you basically will be using it for internet, or word processing, I think it would do quite well. I was amazed to find Notebooks for less than half what I paid for mine 3 years ago. Better monitors, faster, more RAM, bigger HD, etc. It's been a while since I looked at complete computers.||||reg||1
Z||000007||theogre||01-01-2001||09:35 PM||||Having spent hours last week trying to figure out how/why webtv is what it is..... save the money and just get the computer.<P>The WebTV plus box, according to the way I read their specs and things, is better hardware wise and adds some features but still has all kinds of limitations on what you have to do to get pages to work right on it.<P>If you want to better understand some of the issues go into webtv's developers area on their web site and read the stuff in there. It was the documents and developer's posts in that area that made me decide to restrict WebTV to the older style menu in my cave. It's not that I want to deprive people but it's to much work to adjust the whole cave to accomodate the thing. (I'm guessing that since no-one responded to my other post on the webtv/cave subject that you can get in now.....)<P>There are a bunch of issues other than performance and the tiny amount of memory in the "classic" console. The Java and other sections of it's software are also problems. There are a growing number of sites where users of this thing are limited in what they can access simply because the WebTV software can't process the sites. A bunch of other sites are like mine where we've had to do extra work for webtv to get in. Other sites have banned it outright.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Black88GT||01-01-2001||10:18 PM||||While were on the ISP topic (well, sorta) I figured I would share my recent experience.<P>I had been paying 20 bucks a month for a second phone line, and 25 bucks a month for AOL.  AOL was getting worse and worse, it froze a lot for me, very slow, never got busy signals but it was still very slow and unreliable.  I heard about this new @home cable modem thing, they offered free install (running a cable line to my computer, I could have done that) and it was only 40 bucks a month, first 3 months free.  I figured I would give it a try.  All I can say is, WOW.  It has made the internet exeprience much better for me to say the least.  it loads the biggest pages almost instantaneously, and I can download Napster songs in about 30 seconds as opposed to 30 min., if the other user didn't cancel the upload which they often did.  My record so far has been 227 k/s, on average I am getting 50-75 k/s, but thats still hell of an improvement over 3-4 k/s.  <P>The program here is run by my cable company, Comcast.  If they have it in your area I would highly recommend it, especially if you are tired of dial ups.  Thats another good thing, its always on, and the best feature of AOL, the instant messenger, can be downloaded and used for free.  It's a win-win situation.<P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>'88 GT t-top 3.4<BR>Baltimore, MD||||reg||14
Z||000009||stimpy||01-02-2001||12:48 AM||||Another option is to go with DSL.  I have looked at the "computer rebate"thing that requires a2-3 year commitment to a dial-up. SBC (Southwestern Bell) and Compaq have a deal goig with a 2 yr commitment to DSL at $40/mo + $20 for 2 yrs gets you a Presario computer.Check it out at swbell.com||||reg||1
Z||000010||fiero56||01-02-2001||12:54 AM||||Problem with the cable modem setup is the security issue.||||reg||1
Z||000011||rogergarrison||01-02-2001||11:52 AM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Philphine:<BR><B>roger, do you think it would be worth getting even if i tried to get a computer later anyway? for the tv options? do you end up paying for interet service twice or maybe have it free on the computer?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I like the quick program guide for vcr, I can just type in autoracing and it lists everything on for the next week. Also like the ability to snag stills right from video. but other than that, i use my laptop mostly<P>it runs $24.95 @ month. ya ive heard aol sucks. webtv is getting worse and worse too for net surfing, there upgrades are usually refered to as 'degrades' by users<BR><p>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 01-02-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000012||Alex4mula||01-02-2001||03:18 PM||||I have the WebTV+ for 2 years now. At the begining was nice and fast. Latest upgrades are more like downgrades to me too. The thing I hate most is paying $25 monthly and being forced to watch a WebTV add on the first connection. Have complained to them but to no avail. The "view" buttom does not cancel it on first boot up. I'm very close to ditch the box and get a real PC for the same monthly payment from PeoplePc.com . Try that instead of WebTV. The main thing I like from WebTV is that at least for me it always (99.9%) connects on first trial. ||||reg||1
Z||000013||fiero56||01-02-2001||04:37 PM||||Dial up sucess depends 99% on your ISP. The other 1% is mainboard and modem quality from what I've seen/expericnced. <P>I'm using siscom(local Dayton dialup) and the only time it took more than one dialup to connect was when they had some type of hardware problem. I have a couple of family members who work at Concentric. They gave me a free account for e-mail, internet access, etc. I could never log in, connections always froze, it was the biggest waste of time. I decided to pay $20/month for Siscom than use Concentric for free. I will never pay for Concentric service, ever.||||reg||1
